1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,186
In this video we're going to learn how
to use the Tiny Tapeout GitHub template

2
00:00:04,346 --> 00:00:08,000
to turn your design into the ASIC
files we need to manufacture the chip.

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,000
If you don't already have a GitHub
account you'll need to create one.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,830
Then navigate to one of the template
repositories.

5
00:00:15,143 --> 00:00:19,176
We have one for Wokwi designs and
another one for Verilog or other HDLs.

6
00:00:19,703 --> 00:00:22,000
I'll use the Wokwi template for now.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,236
Click the "Use this template" button. This
creates your own copy of the repository.

8
00:00:29,171 --> 00:00:31,598
For open-source projects
GitHub pays for the

9
00:00:31,622 --> 00:00:34,503
cloud compute which we
use to make the ASIC files.

10
00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:37,076
It's turned on by default
but we still need to

11
00:00:37,183 --> 00:00:39,483
allow the actions to
create the results page.

12
00:00:40,056 --> 00:00:43,376
We have the instructions here but I'll
show you how to do it now.

13
00:00:43,843 --> 00:00:48,811
Go to your repository settings page,
"Pages", then set build and deploy source

14
00:00:48,835 --> 00:00:50,483
to "GitHub actions".

15
00:00:51,436 --> 00:00:56,130
Now we're ready to adapt the template to our
own projects by editing the info.yaml file.

16
00:00:56,863 --> 00:01:00,581
For Wokwi projects you need to
change the project's Wokwi ID to match the

17
00:01:00,605 --> 00:01:02,096
number in your Wokwi project.

18
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:07,536
For HDL projects like Verilog or Amaranth
we use the source file section here.

19
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:11,183
That's covered in a separate video
called "Working with an HDL".

20
00:01:12,036 --> 00:01:15,236
Okay so let's set the Wokwi ID to
match our project.

21
00:01:15,729 --> 00:01:20,000
I'll copy the last part of the projects
URL and replace the 0 with it.

22
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,316
Most simple designs will fit into one
tile.

23
00:01:23,776 --> 00:01:27,690
If you need more space you can change
the tile setting to get more space.

24
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:30,000
Bigger designs cost more.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,303
If you're not sure just start with one
and see what happens.

26
00:01:33,943 --> 00:01:36,870
We also need to fill out the title,
author and so on.

27
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:41,376
If you use Discord then put your Discord
name here and when you submit a design

28
00:01:41,576 --> 00:01:43,256
we'll add you to the Tapeout role.

29
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:46,929
Further down in the file there's a
section to describe what the pins do.

30
00:01:47,116 --> 00:01:50,943
And this is really useful for testing other
people's designs so please fill it in.

31
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:54,000
Then hit the commit button to save your
changes.

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,563
There's one more file you have to edit,
the info.md inside the docs directory.

33
00:02:00,163 --> 00:02:02,236
This is important for a few reasons.

34
00:02:02,429 --> 00:02:06,208
First is that because everyone who
orders a chip will also get your design.

35
00:02:06,382 --> 00:02:09,000
It's nice if they can understand how to
experiment with it.

36
00:02:09,635 --> 00:02:14,000
The second reason is that by the time the chips come
back you'll probably have forgotten how it works

37
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,376
and you'll be glad you wrote down some
instructions.

38
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
You can make the documentation better by adding
pictures and links to extra documentation.

39
00:02:22,376 --> 00:02:25,276
After filling in all the details press
the commit button.

40
00:02:25,723 --> 00:02:27,703
This starts the GitHub actions running.

41
00:02:28,276 --> 00:02:31,523
You can access the results using the
actions tab at the top here.

42
00:02:32,136 --> 00:02:33,543
We have three actions.

43
00:02:33,663 --> 00:02:37,403
One for documentation that shows how your
documentation will look when it's printed

44
00:02:37,736 --> 00:02:42,483
and other will be used for automatic testing of
Wokwi designs if you've used the truth table option.

45
00:02:42,883 --> 00:02:47,240
And finally we have the GDS action
which is used to build the ASIC files for

46
00:02:47,265 --> 00:02:48,000
manufacturing.

47
00:02:48,423 --> 00:02:52,390
If you want to find out more about that we
have a separate video showing how it works.

48
00:02:52,496 --> 00:02:56,000
It's called "How the GitHub action
creates the ASIC files".

49
00:02:56,556 --> 00:02:58,576
Let's take a quick look at the docs
action.

50
00:02:58,902 --> 00:03:01,226
This is useful to see how
your documentation will

51
00:03:01,252 --> 00:03:03,576
be formatted for the
online and print data sheet.

52
00:03:04,156 --> 00:03:06,743
We get a PDF file we
can download and check to

53
00:03:06,768 --> 00:03:08,643
see if the formatting
and the picture works.

54
00:03:09,590 --> 00:03:11,930
Let's take a look at the GDS action
results.

55
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:14,413
We have this nice page
with some warnings,

56
00:03:14,513 --> 00:03:17,766
utilization statistics
and standard cell usage.

57
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:21,658
If you don't understand a warning
join us on the Discord chat server

58
00:03:21,683 --> 00:03:23,610
and post in the
GitHub actions channel.

59
00:03:24,523 --> 00:03:28,000
You can take a look at the cells in use
by clicking these links.

60
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
For example this X-NOR2 gate.

61
00:03:30,910 --> 00:03:33,000
Let's take a closer look at the 3D
viewer.

62
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,948
Right click to pan, left click to change
the perspective,

63
00:03:37,248 --> 00:03:39,000
scroll wheel to zoom in and out.

64
00:03:39,416 --> 00:03:42,375
On the right we can see which
cells are used and how many,

65
00:03:42,722 --> 00:03:45,150
above we have the
control for layer visibility.

66
00:03:46,051 --> 00:03:47,975
We also have some shortcut keys on the
right.

67
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,638
Pressing "1" hides all the
filler cells, "2" hides the wiring

68
00:03:52,663 --> 00:03:55,088
and "3" isolates
the currently selected cell.

69
00:03:55,795 --> 00:03:57,953
I'd really appreciate it
if you took a screenshot

70
00:03:57,977 --> 00:04:00,000
of your design and
posted it on social media

71
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,495
with a description of what it does
along with a Tiny Tapeout hashtag.

72
00:04:04,608 --> 00:04:07,682
Back on the front page of the
repository you'll see we have these

73
00:04:07,707 --> 00:04:11,385
three green lights now for the
docs, test and GDS all passing.

74
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
The GDS status needs to be green to
submit your design.

75
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,816
Sometimes the GDS action will fail.

76
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,329
A Wokwi design should almost always
work.

77
00:04:20,483 --> 00:04:22,988
If not then it's probably our
problem and you should let

78
00:04:23,012 --> 00:04:25,516
us know in the Wokwi channel
of the Discord chat server.

79
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:29,303
If you're using an HDL it's
usually because of an error

80
00:04:29,383 --> 00:04:31,887
in your design or because
the design is too large.

81
00:04:32,347 --> 00:04:36,016
To get more details you can look
at the logs here in the action results

82
00:04:36,186 --> 00:04:40,000
or download the zipped GDS logs
out of hacked and take a look inside.

83
00:04:40,383 --> 00:04:42,816
First check the
synthesis log file under

84
00:04:42,841 --> 00:04:48,716
runs > wokwi >logs > synthesis >
synthesis.errors for design errors.

85
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:51,746
If your design is too large
then the failure will normally

86
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:54,490
be to do with the placement
or routing part of the flow.

87
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,770
You can try to reduce your design
size or upgrade to a larger design

88
00:04:58,976 --> 00:05:02,396
by increasing the number
of tiles in your info.yaml config.

89
00:05:02,883 --> 00:05:05,645
When you're ready to
submit go to tinytapeout.com

90
00:05:05,670 --> 00:05:08,000
and then scroll down to
the submission section.

91
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Click the button to go
to our submission app.

92
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,651
Sign in with your GitHub account
then copy and paste the URL

93
00:05:14,676 --> 00:05:17,463
of your GitHub repository
into the form and press submit.

94
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,556
If you have a coupon you can apply it here
otherwise you'll need to pay to continue.

95
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:26,156
At this point you've created
your project but it's not yet

96
00:05:26,181 --> 00:05:29,496
part of the Tiny Tapeout. You
have to submit a revision first.

97
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,843
Once that's happened that version of
your design is committed to the shuttle.

98
00:05:34,136 --> 00:05:38,000
If you want to make any changes you need to
come back here and submit a new revision.

99
00:05:38,111 --> 00:05:42,111
So that's it. Happy hacking and I look
forward to seeing what you make on your ASIC.

